WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:06.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  dungeons are as essential to the zelda  series as  milk is to cereal from the inception of  the series in 1986 to the present day  dungeons have served to create an  exciting contrast with the world  exploration above ground  back when they were still called levels  the only way to differentiate one from  another was the color palette and the  room layout the music was  identical in each one aside from level 9  and the basic rules and principles also  remained the same  in each one due to the technical  limitations of the nes there weren't a  whole lot of different options when it  came to puzzles aside from  pushing a block defeating a specific  enemy or all enemies in a room  bombing walls an occasional hungry  moblin that needed to be fed or  a flute concert to drive an enemy insane  instead it was the dungeon itself which  served as a puzzle its layout demanded  closely observing your map and figuring  out how to get to the room containing  the boss and the triforce piece  all the while trying to survive the  onslaught of enemies waiting for you  in each room with each new installment  we've seen dungeons evolve from simple  grids to complicated multi-floor pieces  of architecture with each their own  unique look and feel and this was taken  to the next level when the series  finally made its first leap into the  third dimension  three dimensions meant that the layout  of the room and areas was no longer  straightforward  in the 2d games it was very clear where  the entrances or exits were whereas in  three dimensions it's much easier to  miss something because you are not  presented all the information about the  area you just walked in  dungeons can be classified under certain  subgroups based on its traits and its  mechanics  if you've ever seen boss keys you will  know exactly what i'm talking about  lock and key dungeons gauntlets puzzle  boxes etc what this mostly boils down to  is  how you progress through a dungeon do i  need to fight or puzzle my way from room  to room while seldom being presented  with branching paths or do i need to  figure out how the dungeon works  backtrack or generally get lost on my  way to the final goal  and this is what i want to talk about in  this episode linear  versus non-linear dungeon design  if you were to ask the zelda community  as a whole which dungeon is his or her  favorite you would be presented with a  lot of different answers and each with  their own reasons for liking or  disliking a specific one  video game analysts like mark brown king  k and matthew matosis generally lean  more towards complex and non-linear  dungeon design  which is understandable since these are  people who are focused on and interested  in game design and level design but your  average zelda player might not agree  with their assessment since  there is so much more to a dungeon than  just its complexity  atmosphere for instance which is much  more of a subjective aspect can  sometimes make or break a certain  dungeon for people  as an example i've never really liked  the temple of time in twilight princess  and this has nothing to do with the way  it's structured but more to do with my  distaste for pristine and technological  atmospheres when it comes to dungeon  crawling i lean heavily towards the more  classic  old rundown ruins and crypts and dark  dungeons deep dark caves and themes of  horror and decay  which also explains why i like games  like silent hill so much based on this  info i think you can make a pretty  educated guess on what some of my  favorite dungeons in the series are  the forest temple shadow temple and  bottom of the well snow peak ruins  arbiter's grounds and had i played more  of skyward sword i would probably prefer  the ancient cistern if only for this  amazing underworld section  i won't deny that some of my favorite  dungeons aren't necessarily the most  challenging especially in terms of  navigation  but that's not what i play zelda  dungeons for and although i like a good  puzzle  or challenging enemy one of the most  important things for me when entering a  dungeon is  am i excited to be there do i feel a  sense of wonder when i set foot into  this massive structure  now the big difference between a linear  and a non-linear dungeon  is of course the way you navigate it and  reach the boss room  in some dungeons this room is hidden  somewhere deep inside far  far away from the entrance and you have  to soldier your way through to reach it  in others this final goal is right there  in the central room and getting access  to it requires branching out into many  different directions and revisiting the  same areas multiple times  back in the old days of the nes dungeons  which were complex and required  backtracking and crisscrossing through  could be  very confusing to navigate despite the  absence of multiple floors certain warp  points could have you end up in  completely different parts of the maze  and since all the rooms look alike you  often had to use rooms with certain  traits such as a specific block pattern  or other familiarities as anchor points  to know where you have or haven't been  in order to streamline the dungeons in  the 3d installments the level designers  incorporated the central room mechanic  a large space which branches out into  different directions giving the player  something to hold onto while they  explored many rooms and hallways  in the cases of the water temple wind  temple and snow at temple just to name a  few this complexity is  upped even further by including many  different floors to choose from  making the dungeon feel even more  maze-like in order to overcome these  challenges you need to understand how  rooms and floors interconnect and the  relationship between different sections  of the dungeon  this often requires crossing the central  room multiple times as you slowly unlock  the dungeon bit by bit  this also ties into the way the main  dungeon item is incorporated into the  progression  up until the point of obtaining this  item large portions of the dungeon will  be inaccessible and thus you are unable  to get the full picture  in a way this item functions as a  skeleton key for the rest of the dungeon  in linear dungeon design this central  hub mechanic is obviously less necessary  since the path is always going forward  the shadow tempo is perhaps the most  famous or should i say  infamous for its straightforward  progression and while i love this  dungeon to death for its theme  music and atmosphere i do agree that in  terms of navigation the dungeon  could have used some work and by work i  don't mean make it more complex to  navigate since that would completely  ruin the point of the dungeon  it's all about descending deeper and  deeper down after all but if you're  going for a linear style of progression  at least do something original with it  ironically enough the tempo of time  which as i stated before is one of my  least favorite dungeons in twilight  princess does  show how a linear dungeon can be done  right in terms of its navigation  the path is still largely  straightforward but the catch is that  you traverse the same path  twice once on your own and then back the  way you came with the giant statue  which also serves as the key to unlock  the final stretch of the temple  transferring the statue from room to  room and figuring out how to get it into  the correct positions makes for an  interesting concept and also establishes  more of a relationship between the  player and the dungeon itself  from the moment you walk in you know  that the goal is to somehow place a  large statue onto the pedestal in the  first room the entire goal revolves  around it and after you finally arrive  back at the start with the statue the  whole ordeal of getting it there feels  satisfying what's also interesting to  note is that despite its straightforward  navigation it still manages to  incorporate the main progression item  into the linear design in a smart way  since you won't be able to control the  statue without the dominion rod  compare that to the shadow temple which  flat out gives you the main item at the  very start of the dungeon since  well where else are they gonna put it  there's barely any backtracking in the  dungeon as it is  if you don't die on the way to bongo  bongo you will never visit these early  sections ever again  all the dungeons and temples i've  mentioned in these examples so far fall  under a very clear category the water  temple is  obviously non-linear the shadow temple  and temple of time are linear  etc but the majority of dungeons  actually fall more or less somewhere in  between there aren't many dungeons as  complex as the water temple or as linear  as the shadow temple  some might look and feel rather complex  on your first playthrough but when you  actually analyze the correct path you  need to take you quickly come to the  conclusion that there's still  a predetermined route you are allowed to  take the big difference being that  whereas in linear dungeons you don't  have to think about where you have to go  next and are simply experiencing the  dungeon  on a room-to-room basis in these hybrid  dungeons for a lack of a better term  even though the path forward is set in  stone and doesn't require non-linear  exploration  you will sometimes have to backtrack or  accidentally wander into the wrong  direction for a while before getting  stuck and having to turn back  these are basically semi-linear dungeons  sometimes disguised as a non-linear one  as an example in certain dungeons you  can hold multiple keys at once and there  are multiple doors to choose from  each of which takes you into a  completely different section of the  dungeon  in others you also may hold more than  one key at the same time  but the doors with which to unlock them  with are still presented in a  predetermined order giving the illusion  of complexity but actually you are still  just following a set path  and then there's dungeons like the  snowpeak ruins a linear dungeon but  with the layout of a non-linear one for  instance it has a central hub which you  come back to many times  and as you can see from the map it  doesn't exactly look like a linear  experience yet the progression  is completely linear yeta tells you  exactly where to go on your map and only  unlocks doors for you one by one in a  set order  there's literally no way to tackle this  dungeon in any other order than the one  laid out for you  just like with the tempo of time this is  an example of a linear dungeon but with  a unique twist to it which makes it far  more interesting than just going from  room to room in a straight line  plus you get to see link get body slam  to the ground every time you come back  to the main hub i mean  what's not to like there are examples of  dungeons which make use of the same  concept but approach it in a way that  each one feels different  the forest temple and arbiter's grounds  both feature the same mechanic where you  have to light four torches in the  central room by defeating four post  spirits who stole the flame away but the  similarities end with the way you reach  this goal  in arbiter's ground each poke can be  dealt with out of order  right of the bed that presents you with  four different directions to take  in the forest temple this isn't possible  there are two ghosts that can  technically be defeated in a different  order but  these are both in the same general area  and it doesn't change the way you go  about finding your way forward  i think the most important thing to take  from this video is that even though you  might have a favorite dungeon from a  specific  game it's much more important to look at  dungeons in sets  a linear dungeon isn't by definition  inferior to a non-linear one  given that it presents a different type  of challenge for instance through its  combat or its micro challenges like  individual room puzzles the reason why  many people are of the opinion that  twilight princess has some of the best  dungeons in the series  is because each one complements the  other the same thing goes for skyward  sword even though i haven't finished the  game myself  i do hear many voices around me  expressing how unique each dungeon feels  and stands out from one another the  divine beast in breath of the wild are  the very definition of non-linearity and  i think the majority of players agree  that they miss the traditional dungeons  but i have heard some people express  that they actually preferred the divine  beasts and those people  are no better than a deku scrub just  kidding i love you all but my take on  this would be  why not both both both  both is good my problem with the divine  beasts wasn't the way they were  structured but rather that this was the  only dungeon variation we got had breath  of the wild had a divine beast type of  dungeon alongside completely different  ones  it would have stood out and maybe even  have been a fan favorite for many  people but by making all four look play  and even generally sound the same a lot  of people felt underwhelmed  imagine if besides a divine beast we had  completely different dungeons with  different types of progression  some linear some non-linear and some in  between  i have talked about this in my first  video before breath of the wild has  mechanics that are completely new to the  franchise and a divine beast type of  dungeon isn't the only way to make a  brand new dungeon type that has the old  school look and feel of previous games  but which have to be overcome in a  completely new way  linearity can be a great dungeon  experience so does non-linearity the  most important thing is presenting a  solid set  of dungeons which cater to different  people give me that dark cave or  crypt-like experience and give others  that high-tech puzzle box underwater  maze or  ancient temple to explore that way we're  all happy we each have our favorites and  it keeps the overall experience  varied and surprising and with that i  have said what i wanted to say  let me know down in the comments what  your favorite dungeon type is  linear or non-linear are you more into  the dark eerie indiana jones experience  like me  or are you more in favor of the  technological shika shrine vibe or  magical fantasy forest vibe  be sure to let me know so i can shower  you all with hearts  as always a massive thanks to my  gracious patreon supporters who make  these videos possible one of which has  just joined the cause so  say hello to some guy as not to be  confused with  some guy thank you for all your support  and everyone else for watching this  video which believe it or not  also greatly helps i hope you all have a  great weekend and i will see you all  next week this is dawn signing off and  have a good one

